 :doc/body "# Murakumo: Slow, but cheap\n\nOwner decision, 2026-09-08: optimize distributed cluster service for affordable completed work, not minimum first-token latency. Slowness alone is not a service defect. Wrong answers, abandoned requests, hidden retries, incorrect metering and missed agreed deadlines remain defects.\n\nThe intended workload is deadline-tolerant text/document processing and offline generation. A distributed fleet is the architecture direction. The OpenRouter candidate currently qualifies one fixed b70 model backend; this does not demonstrate multi-node model-parallel inference or redundant capacity.\n\nOpenRouter synchronous contract: admit within available capacity; return early 429 with Retry-After on saturation; preserve a stable model identity; send SSE keepalive comments while waiting, with TTFT measured at actual content; propagate cancellation. Do not hide indefinite queues behind a low advertised token price. An asynchronous queued product needs explicit deadlines, cancellation, maximum price, and fair admission.\n\nQualification policy slow-cheap-v2 retains every existing ledger row. Seven days, at least 1000 eligible requests and 500 in each mode, correctness >=99.5%, gaps <=45 minutes, and the tiny-probe 45-second deadline remain. The previous p95 10-second / TTFT 5-second limits were internal preferences, not documented OpenRouter requirements, and are removed. Latency remains measured. Observation completion never sets production-ready or is_ready true.\n\nCheapness has two independent checks: the offered price and sustainable candidate-specific cost. Current proposed catalog pricing is USD 0.10/M input and 0.40/M output. Existing prices.edn cost estimates reference an older sparse model and cannot validate Qwen3.8 dense 27B. Required denominator is successful useful output, counting retry work, electricity, idle time, amortization, operator share and edge/storage fees. Unit economics remain unverified until measured. No cheapest-market or profitable claim is authorized by the concept alone.\n\nLive evidence on 2026-09-08: a long-context stream returned HTTP 200 and an SSE prefill comment after 233 ms; the client cancelled after 234 ms. An immediate origin check showed one busy slot; a later check showed both idle. This proves keepalive delivery and eventual idleness, not prompt cancellation propagation: the underlying request may simply have completed. Cancellation latency, repeated overload/recovery, partner isolation/metering, final data terms and contracting details remain open.\n\nReferences: business-openrouter-awai-murakumo-provider-application; business-openrouter-b70-qualification-evidence-20260908; https://openrouter.ai/docs/guides/community/for-providers.\n"}]
